eral  beneficial effects on aggro ecosystems  more specifically mental cropping has  been shown to have beneficial effects on  pests and disease management and to kind  of give an example i have this data from  solvay at all 2016 where they show that  intercropping onion and lettuce together  um can significantly reduce um pest  pressure um in agroecosystems  and so um so for this project what we  wanted to look at was specifically how  intercropping alters soil properties  because that's kind of received less  attention in the inter-cropping research  and more specifically we wanted to look  at what the effect of inter-cropping was  on soil chemical and biological  properties as well as plant phenotypic  outcomes and production outcomes  and so for this experiment we did a  field trial over at caldwell's field  where we grew tomato plants in one of  three different field treatments so our  first treatment was our control  treatment where we just mono cropped  tomatoes  and then we had two inter-crop  treatments in this experiment so our  first intercrop treatment um consisted  of growing a tomato intercropped with  tobacco and the idea there was to see  what the effect of inter-cropping is  when we grow tomato with a close  solanaceous relative  and then our second inter-crop treatment  consisted of growing uh tomato with kale  and the idea there was to see what the  effect of growing tomato what the more  distantly related brassica crop is  and so we cultivated on these plots and  then throughout the growing season  recorded um soil and plant responses to  inter-crop treatment so look at what the  effect of inter-cropping was on  different soil properties  and in turn on plant phenotypic outcomes  and so i'll start off by uh talking  about some of our soil chemical data so  uh very broadly speaking when we looked  at our soil chemical data we saw several  effects of intercropping on uh on soil  nutrition but the pattern sort of  shifted um with each sort of uh nutrient  that we were looking at so in figure a  we have um soil nitrate and you can see  that soil nitrate is significantly  greater in the tobacco treatment  compared to both the monocrop and kale  treatment  but when we look at other um key soil  nutrients such as phosphorus we see sort  of different patterns emerge  so you can see i'm in figure b that soil  phosphorus is significantly greater in  the kl treatment compared to the tobacco  treatment while the monocrop treatment  again doesn't differ between the other  two  and so what this data is kind of showing  us is that intercropping does have  effects on soil chemistry and the  abundance of these different key plant  nutrients but it's all dependent on crop  combination and what's being cultivated  with one another  um and so in addition to that soil  chemical data we also um did some  microbial sequencing to look at what the  effect was on microbial composition um  so here we're looking at the effects of  treatment and sampling date on microbial  composition  for our sampling days we're specifically  looking at microbial communities at the  start of the experiment versus harvest  and it's kind of hard to see in this  ordination because there is a pretty  large amount of variability in the data  but quantitatively if you look on the  upper right hand side you can see our  permanova and you can see that there are  significant effects of treatment and  date on microbial composition but not  the interaction between the two  and so what this is showing us is that  in addition to altering those different  soil chemical properties um  intercropping also has effects on soil  biological properties and  microbial communities  in  agroecosystems  and then so in addition to looking at  all of that soil data we also looked at  some phenotypic outcomes um so we looked  at traits such as above ground biomass  production foliar nitrogen content and  fruit yields we didn't see any effect of  inter cropping on above above-grou